in february2014 comcast agreed to buy  time warner cable for forty five point  two billion dollars in stock the merger  would combine the nation's two largest  cable operators into a massive company  with about thirty million subscribers  more than triple the amount of its  closest cable competitor and for  regulators those numbers could be a  problem with that many customers the  combined company could have unfair  competitive leverage against TV channels  and other businesses offering online  video streaming like Netflix this is why  antitrust attorneys at the US Justice  Department are leaning towards  recommending the Comcast's bid to buy  Time Warner be blocked one of the  Justice Department's concerns is that  such a large company could unfairly  charge internet video providers which  would ultimately hurt consumers the DOJ  is reviewing Comcast's passive thirty  percent stake and streaming service Hulu  lawyers want to know if comcast was to  actively involved when co-investors Fox  and Disney tried to sell Hulu in 2013  Comcast denies having had any role of  decision not to sell the government  rejection would be a blow to Comcast the  merger is a way for it to fend off  competitors have taken hundreds of  thousands of its TV subscribers in the  past decade Netflix for example had  nearly twice as many subscribers as  Comcast at the end of 2014 another  company affected by the merger charter  communications which lost out to Comcast  a year ago in its own bid to buy Time  Warner Cable if the merger falls through  charter could get another shot
 

